SRC/ioccc/OSK -- six 1990 contest entries, as built for OS-9/68000==================================================================The twelve entries one directory up are as the contest published them, andqueens is built from baruch.c there unchanged.  These are copies of threemore, with the smallest changes that let Microware's C build them.  Eachchange below is every line that differs; `diff' the pair to see them.  bjack.c    NEW.  cmills.hin's compile notes pass four definitions with             -D: M=500 (largest bet), N=52 (one deck), X seeds rand()             from the clock, V is void or int.  This file supplies them             (V=int) and includes cmills.c.  cmills.c   `#include<stdio.h>' becomes `#include <stdio.h>', and the same             for time.h: Microware's cpp rejects the form with no space.  jaw.c      The include gets its space, as above.             `#define P putchar' becomes `#define P(x) fputc((x),stdout)'.             Microware's cpp does not join `P' to the `(' that follows it             in the source ("macro arguments required"); and stdio's             putchar macro evaluates its argument twice when output is             line-buffered, so `P(*--p)' popped two characters whenever             jaw wrote to a pipe or a terminal.  To a file it was right.             The table t and the arrays D and h are declared `remote':             they are 400K of static data, past the 64K the compiler             reaches directly ("value out of range").             The two 10s meaning end of line become '\n', which is CR here;             as 10, atob read the whole input looking for a line end.  scjones.c  The trigraphs are replaced by the characters they stand for,             which is what the contest's own common.mk does with sed for             a compiler without them, and the includes get their space.             What the program PRINTS is unchanged: the original entry,             trigraphs and all.  stdlib.h   EXIT_SUCCESS, EXIT_FAILURE and exit(), as common.mk writes             them.The programs: bjack is cmills.c (blackjack); jaw is jaw.c (zcat forcompress output, and atob when it runs under a name beginning with `a');trigraph is scjones.c (it prints its own source).  dds.c      NEW 2026-09-15.  A BASIC interpreter -- the contest's Best             Language Tool.  Its arrays are declared `remote': E, L, M, P,             B and m[12*999] come to 65,694 bytes together, and l68 will             not place that much where it addresses directly ("non-remote             data allocation ... exceeds 64k").  That one declaration line             is the whole difference from the entry as posted; strtol()             comes from the build driver's own shim.  theorem.c  NEW 2026-09-15.  Best of Show, and four programs in one file:             it declares its globals twice over so that the same source             compiles differently each way it is fed through itself.  l68             takes the second set as a clash, so those are `extern' here.             Nothing it prints depends on that -- it reads its input from             standard input, and the `< theorem.c' in the contest's notes             is a shell redirect, not the program finding its own source.  westley.c  NOT BUILT, and kept here on purpose.  Four changes it needed             are made -- `signed' removed (K&R has none), the three `1s'             short constants its own hint file warns about, its one             indented #define moved to column 1, and putchar through             fputc as jaw needs -- but one line defeats this compiler:             `double time, me= !0XFACE,' runs on across a blank line to             `not;', and the declaration is not carried that far, so             `not' has no type.  Joining those lines builds it and ruins             it: westley won BEST LAYOUT and its source is written to be             read as a letter.  Reformatting the artwork is not a port.  dg.c       NOT BUILT, and kept here to show how far it got.  The entry             defines `d' as `define' and then writes every one of its 60             directives as `#d name(x) ...', relying on the preprocessor             expanding the DIRECTIVE NAME -- which standard C does not do,             so neither cpp here recognised a single definition.  They are             written out as #define in this copy, and index() is strchr().             It still will not build: cc then runs and exits having             written nothing at all, no diagnostic and no module.  The             author expects that of a lesser preprocessor -- his hint file             says compressing the source further earns "defines nested too             deeply".  pjr is not kept: cpp aborts reading it outright.